/-
# Integral Cayley graphs (Kourovka Notebook Problem 19.50)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.50 (D. V. Lytkina): a finite graph is
integral if all eigenvalues of its adjacency matrix are integers. (a) Let a finite group `G` be
generated by a normal subset `R` of involutions. Is the Cayley graph `Cay(G, R)` integral? (b) Let
`S = {(123), (124), …, (12n)}` and `R = S ∪ S⁻¹`. Is `Cay(Aₙ, R)` integral?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Basic.Complex.Basic
public import Mathlib.Combinatorics.SimpleGraph.AdjMatrix
public import Mathlib.Combinatorics.SimpleGraph.Cayley
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.SpecificGroups.Alternating
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Charpoly.Basic

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.IntegralCayleyGraphsKourovka19Problem50Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.50 (D. V.
Lytkina). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701598 (`KOU-19.50`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

`Cay(G, R)` is Mathlib's `SimpleGraph.mulCayley R`: vertex set `G`, `x ≠ y` adjacent when `x⁻¹ y ∈
R` or `y⁻¹ x ∈ R`; here `R = R⁻¹` and `1 ∉ R`, so `x` and `y` are adjacent exactly when `x⁻¹ y ∈ R`
(for symmetric `R` this graph is isomorphic, via `x ↦ x⁻¹`, to the one with `y x⁻¹ ∈ R`). Integral
means every complex root of the characteristic polynomial of the integer adjacency matrix
(`SimpleGraph.adjMatrix`) is an integer. In (b) the points are `Fin n`, `(1 2 k)` becomes `(0 1 k) =
(0 1)(1 k)` with `2 ≤ k < n`, and `n ≥ 3`.
-/

/-- A square integer matrix is integral: every complex root of its characteristic polynomial (every
eigenvalue) is an integer. -/
def HasIntegralSpectrum {n : Type*} [Fintype n] [DecidableEq n] (A : Matrix n n ℤ) : Prop :=
  ∀ μ : ℂ, (A.map (Int.castRingHom ℂ)).charpoly.IsRoot μ → ∃ k : ℤ, (k : ℂ) = μ

open Classical in
/-- Kourovka 19.50(a) (D. V. Lytkina): if a finite group `G` is generated by a normal subset `R`
consisting of involutions, then the Cayley graph `Cay(G, R)` (Mathlib's `SimpleGraph.mulCayley R`)
is integral. -/
def involutionCayleyGraphIntegral : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Fintype G] [DecidableEq G] (R : Set G),
    (∀ r ∈ R, r ^ 2 = 1 ∧ r ≠ 1) → (∀ r ∈ R, ∀ g : G, g * r * g⁻¹ ∈ R) →
    Subgroup.closure R = ⊤ → HasIntegralSpectrum ((SimpleGraph.mulCayley R).adjMatrix ℤ)

/-- The `3`-cycles `(1 2 k)`, `3 ≤ k ≤ n`, in `Sₙ`, written on `Fin n` as `(0 1 k) = (0 1)(1 k)`
with `2 ≤ k < n`. -/
def threeCyclesOneTwo (n : ℕ) : Set (Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) :=
  {σ | ∃ a b k : Fin n, a.val = 0 ∧ b.val = 1 ∧ 2 ≤ k.val ∧ σ = Equiv.swap a b * Equiv.swap b k}

open Classical in
/-- Kourovka 19.50(b) (D. V. Lytkina): for `S = {(123), (124), …, (12n)}` and `R = S ∪ S⁻¹`, the
Cayley graph `Cay(Aₙ, R)` (Mathlib's `SimpleGraph.mulCayley R`) is integral (`n ≥ 3`). -/
def alternatingThreeCycleCayleyGraphIntegral : Prop :=
  ∀ n : ℕ, 3 ≤ n → HasIntegralSpectrum ((SimpleGraph.mulCayley
    {g : alternatingGroup (Fin n) | (g : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) ∈ threeCyclesOneTwo n ∨
      (g : Equiv.Perm (Fin n))⁻¹ ∈ threeCyclesOneTwo n}).adjMatrix ℤ)

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 19.50(a) "Yes" (Revin; Abdollahi) and gives the proof:
every eigenvalue of `Cay(G, R)` has the form `(1/χ(1)) ∑_{r ∈ R} χ(r)` for an irreducible character
`χ`, which is rational because characters are integers on involutions, and an algebraic integer.
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.50 (D. V.
Lytkina); resolution: (a) D. O. Revin, letter of 21 April 2018; A. Abdollahi, letter of 3 May 2018;
(b) W. Guo, D. V. Lytkina, V. D. Mazurov and D. O. Revin, Integral Cayley graphs, Algebra Logic 58
(2019), no. 4, 297-305, doi:10.1007/s10469-019-09550-2, Kourovka Archive, Problem 19.50: (a) Yes,
with a character-theoretic proof in the Archive (each eigenvalue is (1/χ(1)) ∑_{r ∈ R} χ(r),
rational since characters take integer values on involutions); (b) Yes,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted involutionCayleyGraphIntegral_holds : involutionCayleyGraphIntegral

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 19.50(b) "Yes", citing Guo, Lytkina, Mazurov and Revin.
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 19.50 (D. V.
Lytkina); resolution: (a) D. O. Revin, letter of 21 April 2018; A. Abdollahi, letter of 3 May 2018;
(b) W. Guo, D. V. Lytkina, V. D. Mazurov and D. O. Revin, Integral Cayley graphs, Algebra Logic 58
(2019), no. 4, 297-305, doi:10.1007/s10469-019-09550-2, Kourovka Archive, Problem 19.50: (a) Yes,
with a character-theoretic proof in the Archive (each eigenvalue is (1/χ(1)) ∑_{r ∈ R} χ(r),
rational since characters take integer values on involutions); (b) Yes,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted alternatingThreeCycleCayleyGraphIntegral_holds :
    alternatingThreeCycleCayleyGraphIntegral

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.IntegralCayleyGraphsKourovka19Problem50Wanted
